  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe thunderstorms possible again today with a \nfocus south and west of the I-95 metros. Damaging winds are the main \nthreat.\n\nAlready off to a somewhat active start this morning with additional \nscattered strong to severe thunderstorm activity this afternoon. \nTracking several areas of concern this morning which will in turn \nset us for the potential severe weather threat this afternoon. The \nfirst area of concern is a decaying complex of storms ejecting \neastward from north-central WV, southwest PA, and western MD early \nthis morning. Current guidance drags this activity toward the I-81 \ncorridor around 8-10z/4-6am this morning before dissipating it as it \nworks further east toward the metros. WInd gusts up to 40 mph along \nwith small hail, frequent lightning, and locally heavy rainfall will \nbe possible with this clustering of storms especially west of \nI-81. The convective debris/cloud cover leftover from this area \nof storms will help determine how much severe weather we \nactually see this afternoon.\n\nThe next area of concern is a developing cluster of storms up across \nthe upper Midwest. This cluster of storms will slide south and east \nthrough the lower Great Lakes and Ohio River Valley later this \nmorning into midday before surging south and east through \ncentral/southern WV and into central and southwest VA this \nafternoon and evening. Hi-res guidance is all over the place \nwhen it comes to how this system will evolve and impact our \nregion in regards to both timing and coverage of storms based \nupon the convective debris this morning. Some of the guidance \nhas a linear feature working across central WV into southwest VA\nperhaps grazing the southern Shenandoah Valley while other \npieces of guidance have a cluster of storms working across the \nentirety of the forecast area between 19z-23z-3- 7pm. \nAdditionally a secondary batch of showers and thunderstorms may \ncome through during the overnight period into early Wednesday \nmorning similar to what we are seeing now. SPC maintains a \nSlight Risk (Level 2 out of 5) for eastern WV/far western MD and\nthe Shenandoah Valley. A Marginal Risk (Level 1 out 5) has been\noutlooked elsewhere across the region for through tonight. \nDamaging winds will once again be the main threat along with \nisolated instances of flash flooding. \n\nBeyond Tuesday, timing, coverage, and locations of storms remains \nuncertain despite strong mid level flow and increased height falls \nacross the region. Shortwave disturbances will continue to pulse \nthrough although more cloud cover may be noted which could dampen \nthe threat. Machine learning probabilities for severe weather have \ndecreased to around 5-20 percent with a focus further south. These \nprobabilities taper even more late week as the front finally drops \ninto the region. Once again damaging winds will be the main threat \nwith storms along with isolated instances of flash flooding. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms remain possible at\ntimes Wednesday through Friday, with a cooling trend expected \ntoward the weekend.\n\nDaily storm chances look to continue through at least Friday as a \ncold front gradually drifts south. Ahead of that front will be \nseveral fast moving shortwave disturbances which will help aid \nin shower and thunderstorm development during the diurnal \nperiods. The southward progress of the cold front is uncertain\nhowever, and could be modulated by various convective complexes\nand a potential wave of low pressure tracking along it Thursday\nor Friday. Both physics-based and AI guidance show lower severe\nweather chances Wednesday through Friday with declining \ninstability. Showers and thunderstorms could remain possible \nthough with the front nearby. Thursday may be relatively the \nhottest day as heights briefly rise.\n\nFor Saturday in particular, there may be a better chance of \ndrying out as high pressure builds to the north. This will also \nbring a corresponding cool down in temperatures (potentially a \nbit below normal) along with lower humidity. The front won't be \ntoo far to the south however, and will likely attempt to lift \nnorth later this weekend or early next week as a low pressure \nsystem moves into the Great Lakes. Thus, some low end PoPs \nremain in the forecast each day through the end of the forecast \nperiod.",
